{0 Observe}

Observe is a portable structured logging core for OCaml. It owns point and
wide-log authoring, admission, canonical completion, formatting, diagnostics,
additional drains, and deterministic capture without performing I/O.

Point logs auto-emit one text, anonymous structured, or declared record event.
Wide logs use the same field and schema meaning but accept successive lazy
contributions before one explicit or scope-bounded completion. The core owns
current-handle meaning and operation behavior; runtime compositions supply the
dynamic binding and effect settlement required to execute them.
Drains that accept work for later effectful delivery can report a terminal
worker failure through {!val:Observe.Drain.Integration.report_failure} without
recursing through normal logging.

{1 Installation}

Link the portable core with [(libraries observe)]. Add
[(preprocess (pps observe.ppx))] when using the deriver or untyped-value extension.
Configurable Lwt I/O and the ready Unix composition are distributed in the
[observe-lwt] and [observe-lwt-unix] packages.

{1 Authoring}

Ordinary code logs through {!module:Observe.Logs}:

{[
[%observe.info text ~tag:"auth" "User logged in"];

[%observe.debug text ~tag:"query" "%s" (explain_query query)];

[%observe.info
  untyped { action = "user_login"; user_id = 42 }];

[%observe.info typed ~using:event_schema event]
]}

The callback and every text, untyped, or typed value constructed inside it run
only after route and level admission. Before production
initialization, logs outside an active capture are withheld and counted rather
than raising an initialization exception.

A portable wide log is explicit:

{[
let log = Observe.Logs.create ~name:"checkout" () in
[%observe.set log { cart_id = string cart_id }];
[%observe.set log { phase = "authorized" }];
[%observe.info log "authorization completed"];
Observe.Logs.emit log
]}

For a record derived with [@@deriving observe], [event_schema] locks complete
point values and sparse wide patches to the same declared root. The deriver also
generates [event_patch] and one-layer
[[%observe.set log typed { ... }]] authoring.

{1 Configuration and output}

{!val:Observe.Config.create} returns a validated result;
{!val:Observe.Config.create_exn} raises
{!exception:Observe.Config.Invalid_configuration}. Automatic console output is
automatic formatted output. {!module:Observe.Drain} values are additional
application-owned outputs that receive completed {!type:Observe.Log.t} values.
[Config.Silent] suppresses the console, not drains. [Config.Auto] chooses
pretty development output or production NDJSON from the environment.

{1 I/O composition and capture}

{!module-type:Observe.IO.S} owns effects, dynamic binding, cleanup, native
control exceptions, the wall clock, console presentation capability, and
console offers. {!module:Observe.Make} consumes that completed contract without
adding a runtime or operating-system dependency to the core.

{!val:Observe.Make.with_capture} installs capture as the innermost
dynamic route, suppresses production delivery for the callback, restores the
previous binding, and closes the capture exactly once.

{1 Safety boundary}

Every published point or wide log contains one package-owned immutable
snapshot. Canonical freezing applies fixed limits to depth, width, nodes,
strings, bytes, and retained size before capture or output. Caller mutation
after emission cannot change later inspection or delivery. Unsafe, cyclic,
unsupported, invalid, or raising projection paths are withheld and diagnosed
without a live-value fallback. Runtime control exceptions remain native.

Descriptions generated by [@@deriving observe] retain presentation distinctions
that JSON intentionally collapses, including strings versus nullary
constructors and ordinary versus polymorphic constructors. Their bounded direct
freezer creates format-neutral canonical meaning; official formatters consume
only that result. Decoding, comparison, binary encoding, and the remaining
machine operations stay with Repr. {!val:Observe.Type.of_repr} is an explicit
compatibility path and does not imply a safe canonical freezer.

The portable core owns no background writer or durability guarantee. The ready
Lwt-Unix package owns a bounded serialized console worker and exposes explicit
[flush] and [shutdown] lifecycle operations. See the repository README for
ready, test-capture, and custom-integration examples.
